### Is there any evidence that the income of Joe Biden and Jill Biden decreased or remained stable in the years immediately before October 2020, contradicting the claim of an increase?
Answer: Yes, the search result provides evidence that Joe and Jill Biden’s income decreased significantly in the years immediately before October 2020, contradicting the claim of an increase. According to the article, “He and his wife reported income of more than $11 million in 2017 and more than $4.5 million in 2018, much of it from book deals,” while in 2019 they “reported about $945,000 in taxable income,” showing a consistent decline.

Source URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/biden-releases-2019-tax-returns-ahead-first-debate-n1241387

## Final Judgement
The evidence consistently shows a significant decline in the combined income of Joe and Jill Biden from 2017 to 2019: approximately $11 million in 2017, $4.5 million in 2018, and $985,000 in 2019. This directly contradicts the claim that their income increased recently. No evidence indicates any increase in the period leading up to October 2020.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted by the available tax return data, which shows a **consistent and significant decline** in Joe and Jill Biden's combined adjusted gross income: from approximately **$11 million in 2017** ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2019/07/09/joe-biden-releases-tax-returns-during-2020-democratic-primary.html)) to **more than $4.5 million in 2018** ([NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/biden-releases-2019-tax-returns-ahead-first-debate-n1241387)), and further down to **about $985,000 in 2019** ([CPA Practice Advisor](https://www.cpapracticeadvisor.com/2020/09/30/biden-2019-tax-returns-show-high-income-and-high-tax-rate/40527/)). No evidence supports an income increase in the period leading up to October 2020.